<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaaba</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ka'bah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kaaba is a cube-shaped building at the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram">Great Mosque of Mecca</a>, the holiest site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Before Islam it was a shrine housing many idols, visited by pilgrims from across western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. After taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 630 Muhammad cleared away the idols, and Muslims worldwide face the Kaaba in prayer and circle it during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaaba.jpg" alt="Kaaba" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ka%27ba,_Great_Mosque_of_Mecca,_Saudi_Arabia_(4).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Kaaba means cube in Arabic. In the pre-Islamic religions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> many deities were represented by cubic stone carvings or cubic structures regarded as their abodes, with sacred zones around them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a> of the Kaaba has been compared to pre-Islamic stones called baetyls, often black and thought to be of meteoric origin. According to Islamic tradition, the Kaaba was rebuilt several times, notably by Ibrahim and his son Ismail. Scholars disagree about its standing before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>: Christian J. Robin argues that it became prominent in the last decades of the 6th century, while Peter Webb argues that it largely had a local role in Western Arabia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Kaaba is a cuboid stone building at the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram">Masjid al-Haram</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, about 15 metres high, with sides of 12 and 10.5 metres. Inside, the floor is of marble and limestone, and the interior walls are clad with white marble halfway to the roof. Muslims consider it the House of God, and it determines the qibla, the direction of prayer. Early Muslims faced towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">Al-Aqsa Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> before the direction changed to the Kaaba, which Muslims believe followed a Quranic revelation to Muhammad. Pilgrims circle it seven times anticlockwise in tawaf, which is required in both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> and the umrah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Muhammad took part in the reconstruction of the Kaaba around 600 CE, after a fire and then a flood had weakened it. According to Ibn Ishaq, he settled a quarrel among the Meccan clans over who should set the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a> in place by having the elders raise it on a cloak and then setting it with his own hands.</p><p>The building was badly damaged by fire on 31 October 683 during the first siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in the war between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, who rebuilt it. In 693 '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</a> had al-Zubayr's building razed and rebuilt on the foundations laid by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>; its basic shape has not changed since. In 930 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> sacked Mecca and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a> to al-Ahsa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaaba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Islam began in Mecca, a trading town in western Arabia that was home to the <b>Kaaba</b>, a sanctuary to which Arabs came on pilgrimage to honour many gods.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The idols around the <b>Kaaba</b> were destroyed and it was rededicated as the centre of Islamic worship.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Umayyad siege of Mecca, held by Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr against the caliph Yazid I, the <b>Kaaba</b> was severely damaged by fire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-31">31 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He governed from Mecca, where he rebuilt the <b>Kaaba</b> after it was damaged in an Umayyad siege, while his brother Mus'ab ruled Iraq from Basra.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li><li><blockquote>Arab tradition credits Qusayy ibn Kilab with gathering the Quraysh clans in Mecca in the 5th century and taking control of the <b>Kaaba</b>, a shrine already visited by pilgrims from across western Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/pre-islamic-mecca">Pre-Islamic Mecca</a></li></ul></section>
